  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 245 AM Saturday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Heat Advisory across the southern portions of \ncentral NC today. However, high heat and humidity can be expected \nthroughout the region with the heat indices expected to reach 100+ in \nall areas this afternoon. \n\nSatellite data indicated relatively thin cirrus extending across \nthe lower Ohio Valley south and east into NC. The current pattern of \na strong upper ridge centered over AR, with a WNW flow aloft from \nthe Midwest into the Mid-Atlantic states continues. Surface dew \npoints were way up into the mid 70s nearing 80 in the SE this \nmorning. There could be a few stratus to start the day in the east; \nhowever, expect mostly sunny skies with hot temperatures today. A \nweak low level boundary will likely extend from NW to SE across the \nregion this afternoon. Highs will soar into the 90s, even along and \njust north of the boundary. There will even be some moisture pooling \ntoday with dew points expected to remain in the mid 70s. This will \nkeep the Heat Advisory criteria of 105+ degree heat indices likely \nover the southern Piedmont, Sandhills, and portions of the Coastal \nPlain. Elsewhere, no break from the heat expected as heat indices \nshould reach the upper 90s to 102 over the Triad, Triangle, and \nRocky Mount. \n\nAdditional heat advisories are likely Sunday into mid next week, \nwith the potential for Excessive Heat Warnings Monday and Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Scattered strong to severe thunderstorms this \nafternoon and evening, with the higher probabilities from VA and the \nNC mountains south and east across the Triad.  \n\nThe weather pattern mentioned above also continues to favor severe \nstorms possibilities over our region. Strong surface heating is \nexpected with the nearly clear skies this morning along and south of \nthe surface boundary that is expected to be in place over the area \nthis afternoon. The front should extend from NW to SE from roughly \nBoone to the Triad and possibly just south of the Triangle during \npeak heating. Thunderstorms are expected to develop over western NC \n(mountains) early to mid afternoon due to differential heating \n(lift) and low level convergence near the boundary. Additional \nstorms upstream associated with the next MCV may also track from WVA \ninto VA or northern NC with additional boundaries (outflows) from \nmid to late day. At least scattered thunderstorms are expected to \ntrack ENE into central NC aided by the boundaries, MLCapes near 2500 \nJ/kg, and PW's near 2 inches. A few wet microbursts are possible. \n\nThe latest CAMS are not overly aggressive, yet the conditions are \nfavorable for a few severe thunderstorms. \n\nThe chance of storms may linger well into the night along the \nresidual boundaries. The chances appear to shift north, mainly into \nVA and NE NC on Sunday afternoon. \n\nKey Message 3...  Excessive heat possible Monday and Tuesday as \nupper ridge briefly expands eastward over the Carolina's. At least \nAdvisory criteria of 105+ heat indices expected with the potential \nof 110 degrees.\n\nStronger upper ridging arriving from the west should push the best \nchance of thunderstorms well to our north and east early next week. \nAdditional sunshine may boost temperatures to near 100 in some \nareas. Some mixing is expected, but if the dew points do stay \nelevated, especially over the Coastal Plain and eastern Piedmont, \nexcessive and even more dangerous heat may result. Some heat indices \nmay reach or exceed 110 Monday. \n\nMuch more on this potential later in the weekend.",
